The key role of carboxylate ligands in Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF in promoting water dissociation kinetics for effective hydrogen evolution in alkaline media
Cheng-Fei Li b, Ting-Yu Shuai a, Li-Rong Zheng d, Hai-Bo Tang b, Jia-Wei Zhao c, Gao-Ren Li a,
a College of Materials Science and Engineering, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610065, China  b Foshan Xianhu Laboratory of the Advanced Energy Science and Technology Guangdong Laboratory, Xianhu Hydrogen Valley, Foshan 528200, China  c School of Chemistry, Sun Yat-Sen University, Guangzhou 510275, China  d Beijing Synchrotron Radiation Facility, Institute of High Energy Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:  Ni-MOFs  Ru  Carboxylate ligands  Water dissociation kinetics  Hydrogen evolution
The \(\mathbf{M}(\varpi\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}\) metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u}\), Pt, Ir, etc.) have been widely recognized as the outstanding electrocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). However, the mechanism of alkaline HER on \(\mathbf{M}(\varpi\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}\)-MOFs remains ambiguous. Here, taking \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) as an example, its HER synergistic catalytic mechanism has been revealed by in situ FT-IR/Raman spectra, H/D isotope experiments, methanol/ \(\mathrm{^{\prime}S C N^{-}}\) poisoning experiments and DFT calculations. Namely, the carboxylate ligands in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\), apart from regulating the local electronic structure of surface sites, can be used as a Lewis base that exhibits strong affinity toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) via intermediate state of –COO-H-OH and subsequently promotes water transfer/activation/dissociation via the form of –COO–H–O–H-Ru with a low kinetic energy barrier. \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) shows a low overpotential of \(25\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) with a small Tafel slope \((31\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) and excellent stability.
## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen molecular \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) with a clean and promising energy carrier has been widely considered as one of the attractive candidates for the exhausted fossil fuels. [1] For various \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-production technologies, the electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water splitting, as a carbon-zero process for producing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), has attracted great interest. To realize high-efficiency hydrogen production, the high active electrocatalysts are critical to accelerate sluggish hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) kinetics and reduce overpotential. [2] It is well known that the dissociation of water is a rate-determining step for most of electrocatalysts for alkaline hydrogen evolution. [3–4]. Thus, how to develop the electrocatalysts to effectively break hydrogen–oxygen bond of water with a low dissociation energy is very important. Although Pt-based catalysts exhibited superior catalytic performance for alkaline HER, the high cost, scarcity and instability (irreversible aggregation of Pt nanoparticles) limit their large-scale practical applications. [5].
Recently, great efforts have been devoted to developing the promising alternatives to Pt-based catalysts, such as phosphates, [6] carbides, [7] oxides, [8] nitrides, [9] metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)-based composites [10–11] and sulfides. [12–13] Especially, the metal@MOFs composites as catalysts show great potential for HER in neutral or
Recently, great efforts have been devoted to developing the promising alternatives to Pt-based catalysts, such as phosphates, [6] carbides, [7] oxides, [8] nitrides, [9] metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)-based composites [10–11] and sulfides. [12–13] Especially, the metal@MOFs composites as catalysts show great potential for HER in neutral or alkaline media. [14–17] However, these works about the MOFs-based catalysts always ignored an important issue during HER process. Namely, the critical role of organic ligands of MOFs on the reaction kinetics remain ambiguous. In addition to the optimized binding strength of A-H (A denotes as active site) by electronic synergistic modification between metal nanoparticles and MOFs, how carboxylate ligands promote adsorption, transfer, activation, dissociation of water molecules have not been investigated yet. It is well known that the Lewis bases, such as \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}/\mathrm{COO}^{-}/\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}\), [18–21] as a typical proton acceptor, are often used as a transfer station for protons and even perform the functions of water activation and dissociation. Especially in the water transport of biological tissues, the carboxyl group as a promoter from amino-acid resides of protein backbones can perform the internal base for proton transfer by the form of hydrogen bond with coordinated water. [20] Therefore, inspired by many previous studies, the reasons for remarkably superior reaction kinetics and accompanying much low overpotential for MOF-based catalysts need to be further investigated. Namely, the relationship of water dissociation rate and the functions of organic ligands should be elucidated deeply. As shown in scheme 1, compared with direct dissociation of water on the individual catalyst (left), the carboxylate ligand as an efficient cocatalyst will help catalyst to realize a lower energy pathway to synergistically cleave H–OH bond
of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (right).
To confirm the above idea, \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) electrocatalyst was synthesized via one-step hydrothermal method by spontaneous redox reaction and coordination reaction. Various physicochemical characterization techniques including in situ Raman spectrum, in situ Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy, \(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}\) isotope experiments and theory calculations revealed that the uncoordinated carboxylic groups in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) not only favor initial water adsorption, accelerate transfer of water molecule, but also synergistically facilitate the dissociation of water on the adjacent Ru active sites. The \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) with \(\sim1.3\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) Ru exhibited the outstanding reaction kinetics \((31\ \mathrm{mV\ dec}^{-1})\) with much lower \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) dissociation energy \((0.48\ \mathrm{eV})\) than that of pure Ru nanoparticles (NPs) \((0.84\ \mathrm{eV})\), and it showed the remarkable catalytic performance for HER in alkaline media with a low overpotential of \(25\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and excellent durability with \(8\ \mathrm{mV}\) loss in overpotential after \(80\ \mathrm{h}\) (i). As expected, the above results will bring two important concepts: (i) organic functional groups as Lewis base providing a transfer station of proton will provide a guidance for the design of cost-effective and steady catalyst; (ii) An available pathway will be developed to break the dilemma that HER catalytic activity for most of the catalysts in alkaline medium are usually-two to three orders of magnitude lower than that in acid solution ascribed to the difficult dissociation step of water.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF
A mixed \(30\ \mathrm{mL}\) N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solution containing ethanol \((1.5\ \mathrm{mL})\), water \((1.5\ \mathrm{mL})\), benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) \((80.0\ \mathrm{mg})\), \(\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((0.003\ \mathrm{g})\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((0.140\ \mathrm{g})\) were transferred into \(45\ \mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined autoclave. Subsequently, a piece of clean Ni foam (NF) with \(2.5\ \mathrm{cm}\times2.8\ \mathrm{cm}\times1\ \mathrm{mm}\) was placed into the above Teflon-lined autoclave, and then was maintained at \(125\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\) in an oven. Finally, the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) was grown on the surface of NF, washed with ethanol and water, and dried at room temperature.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni-MOFs/NF
Ni-MOFs was synthesized with the same procedures as above, except that without \(\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\).
## 2.3. Physical characterizations
The structures and morphologies of samples were analyzed by transmission electron microscopy (JEOL ARM200 at \(300\ \mathrm{kV}\)), scanning
The structures and morphologies of samples were analyzed by transmission electron microscopy (JEOL ARM200 at \(300\ \mathrm{kV}\)), scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Quanta 400FEG instrument), and X-ray diffraction (Rigaku SmartLab diffractometer with \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha\) \((\lambda=1.5406\ \mathrm{\AA})\) radiation operating at \(30\ \mathrm{kV}\) and \(200\ \mathrm{mA}\). Surface chemical components and valence states of various samples were recorded by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (ESCA Lab250 X-ray photoelectron spectrometer). Fourier Transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of various samples were carried out by FT-IR instrument (EQUINOX 55). The Raman spectroscopy was conducted on a RENISHAW in Via instrument using an excitation wavelength of \(532\ \mathrm{nm}\). The procedure of FT-IR spectra measurement is shown as following: \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) was firstly dried in vacuum oven at \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). Then the resultant \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) was immersed in \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution for \(5\ \mathrm{min}\), respectively, and obtained \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) was marked as \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs-D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). After that, the above two samples were dried at \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) and then measured in FT-IR instrument.
## 2.4. Electrochemical characterizations
Electrochemical measurements were conducted in a three-electrode cell with an electrochemical station (CHI 760E). The samples, graphite rod and \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) electrode were used as working electrode, counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. All polarization curves were corrected with \(100\ \%\) ir-compensation and obtained at a scan rate of \(5\ \mathrm{mV\ s}^{-1}\) in solution of \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}\). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements of all samples were recorded at \(-1\ \mathrm{V}\) vs \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) with a frequency range from \(100\ \mathrm{K\ Hz}\) to \(0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}\).
The turnover frequency (TOF) was evaluated by the following standard equation:
\[
TOF=\frac{J\times A}{2\times F\times n}
\]
here, \(J\) is the current density \((\mathrm{A\ cm}^{-2})\) at an overpotential of \(0.15\ \mathrm{V}\); \(A\) is the area of electrode; \(n\) is the moles of active metal atoms; \(F\) is the Faraday constant \((96485\ \mathrm{C\ mol}^{-1})\).
2.5. Computational methods.
Vienna Ab Initio Package (VASP) was employed to carry out all the density functional theory (DFT) calculations within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) by using the PBE formulation. [22] We have chosen the projected augmented wave (PAW) potentials to describe the ionic cores and take valence electrons into account by using a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff of \(500\ \mathrm{eV}\). [23] Partial occupancies of Kohn-Sham orbitals were allowed by using Gaussian smearing method and a width of \(0.05\ \mathrm{eV}\). The electronic energy was considered self-consistent when the energy change was smaller than
\(10^{-6}\,\mathrm{eV}\). A geometry optimization was considered convergent when the force change was smaller than \(0.02\ \mathrm{eV/\mathring{A}}\). Grimme's DFT-D3 methodology was used to describe the dispersion interaction. During the structural optimization, the \(2 \times 2 \times 1\) Monkhorst-Pack k-point grid for Brillouin zone was utilized for k-point sampling, and all atoms were allowed to relax. Finally, the free energy for our system was calculated. In addition, the U corrections were set as 3.78 and \(4.15\,\mathrm{eV}\) for Ru and Ni atoms, respectively, in our system. [24] In our calculations, the Free energy was obtained by \(\mathrm{G}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{total}}+\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{ZPE}}\)-TS, where \(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{total}}\), \(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{ZPE}}\), and TS are the ground-state energy per unit cell, zero-point energy, and entropy term, respectively, with the latter two taking vibration frequencies from DFT.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalyst synthesis and characterizations
The synthetic procedure for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) is illustrated in Fig. 1a, in which the nickel foam (NF) not only serves as the conductive substrate to support the catalysts, but also as reducing agent to efficiently reduce \(\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}\) ions to metallic Ru, due to a thermodynamically favorable process \((\Delta E=E_{\mathrm{Ru(III)/(0)}}-E_{\mathrm{Ni(III)/(0)}}>0, E_{\mathrm{Ni(II)/(0)}}=-0.257\,\mathrm{V},\)
\(\mathrm{E_{Ru(III)/(0)}=+0.704\ V)}\). [25] Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) coordinated with terephthalic acid to form Ni-MOFs nanosheets. Benefiting from two simultaneous reaction process, Ru NPs incorporated into Ni-MOFs and in situ self-assembly grew on the surface of NF (denoted as \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\)). Fig. 1b displays scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of three-dimensional smooth Ni-MOFs nanosheets assembling on NF (denoted as Ni-MOFs/NF), which shows the interconnected networks contributing to electrolyte penetration and especial more exposure of Ru active sites. [26] Surprisingly, after \(\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}\) introduction, Ni-MOFs/NF nanosheets became very rough, suggesting that Ru NPs can change the morphology of Ni-MOFs (Fig. 1c). Furthermore, a typical transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) shows the uniform distribution of Ru NPs with an average diameter of about \(4\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1d). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) in Fig. 1e exhibits the lattice fringes with interplanar spaces of \(0.23\ \mathrm{nm}\), ascribed to the (1 0 0) plane of Ru. Importantly, a well-defined interface structure with a strong interaction for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) can be clearly seen. As reported works, [27–28] the performance of a heterogeneous catalysis is strongly dependent on surface and interface properties, because the interfacial structure can electronically modify the active sites to adjust the bonding strength of reaction intermediates for the improved sluggish HER kinetics. In addition, the crystal structure of
Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF was confirmed by the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure S1), which indicates that Ni-MOFs are isostructural to Ni-based MOFs (CCDC: 985792). However, there is no evident characteristic peak of Ru NPs because of small diameter and low loading, consistent with most reported literatures. [29] The existence of elemental Ru was further confirmed by high angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images, together with STEM-energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) images and energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) image of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF in Fig. 1f-j and Figure S2, demonstrating the elements of Ni, Ru, C and O distributed uniformly. The mass loading of Ru was also determined to be as low as \(1.3\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES), and the Ru/Ni atomic ratio is 0.03, consistent with XPS and EDX results (Figure S3).
To further investigate the chemical composition and surface electronic structure of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed. XPS survey spectra of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF exhibits coexistence of Ni, Ru, O and C elements (Figure S4), in good agreement with ICP-AES and EDS analysis results. As seen from Fig. 2a, high-resolution Ru 3p spectra of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF shows Ru \(3\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(3\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) peaks at 463.2 and \(485.4\,\mathrm{\eV}\), respectively, which are assigned to metallic Ru. Notably, the binding energy of Ru \(3\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) upshifts by \(0.6\,\mathrm{eV}\) compared with the fresh Ru/Ni-MOFs/NF catalyst (prepared by physical mixing of Ru NPs and Ni-MOFs. and subsequently loading on the NF), indicating the interracially electronic effect between Ru NPs and Ni-MOFs makes Ru partially positively charged. Interestingly, it is clear that the peaks at \(465.5\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(487.6\,\mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to Ru-O bonds, [30] suggesting that Ru NPs can be stably anchored to the ligand frameworks via the formation of interfacial bonds between the edge of Ru NPs and carboxyl oxygen of Ni-MOFs, which will obviously enhance long-term catalytic stability. Surprisingly, the O 1 s spectra of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF demonstrates a typical characteristic peak of Ru-O at 530.9 eV as shown in Fig. 2b, [14] further confirming the formation of Ru-O bond. In addition, other three peaks could be assigned to Ni-O (531.3 eV), O=C-O (531.8 eV) and C-O/H2O (533.0 eV). Notably, compared to Ni-MOFs/NF, the O 1 s peak exhibited negative shift after Ru adding,
especial Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF, demonstrating strong electronic interaction between Ru and Ni-MOFs. For the high-resolution C 1 s spectrum (Figure S5), the peak at binding energies of 284.6, 285.7 and \(288.5\,\mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to C-C bond, C=C bond and carboxylate group (O=C-O), respectively, from organic ligands. Evidently, compared with Ni-MOFs/NF, the signal at \(281.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) ascribed to Ru NPs can be observed for Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF, suggesting the existence of elemental Ru, consistent with the mentioned above element mapping analyses. As expected, the binding energies of carbon-containing species is essentially unchanged in all samples. It should be noted that the peak position of Ni 2p shows slightly negative shift after Ru NPs incorporation compared with that of Ru/Ni-MOFs/NF (Fig. 2c).
X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscope (XAFS) also revealed the chemical state and local coordinated structure of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF. As shown in Fig. 2d, the X-ray-adsorption energy near edge structure (XANES) of Ru k-edge spectra of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF demonstrates higher absorption energy than that of Ru/Ni-MOFs/NF, meanwhile located between Ru foil and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), implying that Ru is positively charged in Ru@Ni-MOF/NF, and also verifying the strong electron interactions between Ru NPs and Ni-MOFs, consistent with XPS analytic results. The Fourier transforms of XAFS (FT-XAFS) spectra exhibit a predominant peak at ca. 1.6 and \(2.3\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF, assigning to the Ru-O and Ru-Ru scatting paths (Fig. 2e). By contrast, \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), Ru/Ni-MOFs and Ru foil only display a dominant Ru-O or Ru-Ru path, demonstrating the formation of Ru-O-Ni interfacial bond at the Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF that is beneficial for anchoring the Ru NPs to avoid aggregation and promote interfacial electron transfer. Interestingly, compared to \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), a negative shift of Ru-O peak exhibit shorter bond length resulting in distorted Ru-O coordination in Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF, as further evidenced by Ru K-edge \(\mathbf{k}^{3}\chi\) data of EXAFS oscillations (Fig. 2f). Notably, Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF exhibits less amplitude oscillations compared with Ru/Ni-MOFs/NF, indicating the surface structure reforming of Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF. The above comparisons highlight that carboxylate ligands can regulate electronic structure of Ru via the formation of Ru-O interfacial bonds, and the immobilization of Ru NPs on the surface of Ni-MOF nanosheets can be successfully realized via the Ru-O interaction.
## 3.2. The role of carboxyl group for HER
Apart from the role of carboxylate ligands on the electron modification to metallic Ru, which is actually universal in similar numerous previously reported works, [14–17] the role of carboxyl group \(\left(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\right)\), as a Lewis base used to accept the proton, on the adsorption/transfer/activation/dissociation of water molecular is rarely reported for HER in alkaline media. Herein, taking \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) as an example, we firstly investigated the functional groups in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) via FT-IR spectrum. As shown in Fig. 3a, \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) has demonstrated the
symmetric and antisymmetric stretching vibrations of the carboxylate groups of ligands coordinated with metal atoms at 1392 and \(1593\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). [31] Of note, compared with pure Ni-MOFs/NF, a typical sharp absorption peak related to undissociated carboxylic acid group at 1638 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be seen, reflecting the presence of free ligand in the as-obtained \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\). [32] That is, the free carboxylic acid group exposing on the surface of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) would be used as the transfer station of proton to adsorb water molecular in the form of Ar-COO-H-OH in alkaline electrolyte. This is why the oxygen groups based catalysts show good wetting property. [33–34] Furthermore, compared
with the adsorption peak of \(\mathrm{Ni-O}\) at \(590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in Ni-MOFs/NF, the broad stretching peak shifting to lower frequencies in Ru@Ni-MOFs/NF indicates the formation of Ru-O bond excepting for Ni-O bond between the metal atoms and carboxylic group of terephthalate. [14–15].
In order to demonstrate above assumption of the adsorption of water molecular via the uncoordinated carboxylic groups, the deuterium kinetics isotope effects (KIEs) were performed by using the different solvent \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) isotopic study (Fig. 3b and 3c, for detail conditions of testing, please see the experimental section in the supporting information). Obviously, the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF-D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) shows a typical adsorption peak at \(\sim2489~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ascribed to \(\nu(\mathrm{CO-D})\) due to the deuteration of COO– by \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules \(\{-\mathrm{CO-O-D(O)}\}\) as shown in Fig. 3b, [35–36] further confirming that the free uncoordinated carboxylic group can well adsorb water molecules via the formation of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bond (Ar–COO–H(HO)). Furthermore, two distinct characteristic peaks at 1433 and \(1644~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) resulting from -COOD can be observed, further proving the above assumption. [37–38] Surprisingly, the peak ratio of COOD/COOH at \(1433~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) increased, indicating that \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was successfully adsorbed. Moreover, in order to study the adsorption of hydrogen proton via the uncoordinated carboxylate ligand during HER process, in situ FT-IR measurement was carried out at \(-1.0\,\mathrm{V}\) vs \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) as shown in Fig. 3c. Interestingly, the similar characteristic peaks can be obviously seen, indicating that the carboxylate ligand as Lewis base can be used as transfer station of proton to adsorb water molecular. Specifically, the peak intensity of COOH(HO) decreases compared with the corresponding characteristic peaks from ex situ FT-IR spectrum (Fig. 3b), suggesting H of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) chemically coupled Ru site in form of ArCOOH—O—H—Ru, resulting in weak stretching vibration of COOH. [39] Furthermore, it should be noted that the vibration peak of OH of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) shifts to high frequency direction from \(3282~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (ex situ FT-IR spectrum, Fig. 3b) to \(3308~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (in situ FT-IR spectrum, Fig. 3c), suggesting that the H of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was anchored to Ru NPs during HER process. Similar results can be seen in ex situ and in situ FTIR spectra of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF-D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and the adsorption peak of OD of \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in ex situ FT-IR spectrum shifts from 3368 to \(3372~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) compared with that of in situ FT-IR spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF-D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Together with above all results, it can be concluded that the carboxylic group can be utilized as a transfer station for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) via the form of Ar–COO-H(HO), and subsequently perform synergistical activation/dissociation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) via the form of Ar–COO—H—O—H-Ru, thus facilitating proton transmission rate, reducing dissociation energy and promoting sluggish HER reaction kinetics.
In order to further verify the sites of \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)and Ru are involved in hydrogen evolution process, the poisoning experiments of methanol and \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)were conducted. As shown in Figure S6a, the HER catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) dramatically decreases after the addition of methanol because the uncoordinated carboxylic group coordinated with methanol molecular in the form of \(-\mathrm{COO-H-OCH}_{3}\) and it was
prevented from absorbing water, [40] while the polarization curve of \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\) is almost coincident without any catalytic activity decline after the addition of methanol (Figure S6b). So the above results further consolidate the evidence of proton transfer station for carboxylic group. Meanwhile, the adjacent Ru NPs as main active sites are confirmed by \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)that owns a strong nucleophilic ability. [41] After \(\mathrm{SCN}^{-}\)addition, the catalytic activity of Ru NPs is extremely poor (Figure S7a), suggesting that the Ru site was severely poisoned. The similar results can be observed for \(\mathrm{Ru/C}\) catalyst (Figure S7b). So the above results demonstrate the catalytic site is Ru site and \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\)as cocatalyst site can promote the dissociation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) for efficient HER in alkaline media.
evenly reported excellent HER electrocatalysts (left, Fig. 4c and Table S1). When the current density was increased to \(500\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), the overpotential of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) is only \(160\,\mathrm{mV}\), while other pure catalysts and even \(\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}\) all exhibit much higher overpotentials, suggesting the key role of carboxylate ligands on \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) activation and dissociation. In addition, the electrochemically accessible surface area (ECSA) normalized LSV curves are shown in Figure S10, which are employed to highlight the intrinsic catalytic activity (the ECSA is determined by double-layer capacitance \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\) from cyclic voltammetry as shown Figure S11-12). It is obvious that the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) exhibits higher HER intrinsic catalytic activity than \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\), Ni-MOFs/NF and \(\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}\) catalysts. Furthermore, \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) also exhibits much higher \(\mathrm{Pt/Ru}\)-based mass catalytic activity than the other catalysts as shown in Figure S13.
As we all know, reaction kinetics can provide an important insight
into HER reaction pathways on the electrocatalysts. The \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) exhibits a remarkable low Tafel slope of only \(31\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1}\) (Fig. 4b), which is much lower than those of Ni-MOFs/NF \((169\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\) \((60\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}\) \((40\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\) and even most previously reported catalysts (right, Fig. 4c and Table S2). Such a low Tafel slope of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) compared with \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\) suggests that the rate-restricted Volmer step is changed by carboxylate ligands. Additionally, the smaller electrochemical impedance of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) \((6.8\,\Omega)\) than those of \(\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}\) \((31.0\,\Omega)\), \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\) \((35.0\,\Omega)\) and Ni-MOFs/NF \((60.6\,\Omega)\) further manifests a faster HER kinetic process. The turnover frequency (TOF) value of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) was up to \(1.74\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) at an overpotential of \(100\,\mathrm{mV}\), which was much better than those of Ni-MOFs/NF \((0.003\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{Ru/C/NF}\) \((0.23\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}\) \((0.20\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) (Fig. 4e). it is confident to conclude that the interfacial carboxylic group makes the sluggish Volmer step of HER facile in alkaline media. In order
to exclude the effect of nickel foam on HER kinetics enhancement, we investigated catalytic performance of the fresh Ru NPs with and without the carboxylate ligands in 1 M KOH alkaline electrolyte. As shown in Figure S14, Ru NPs manifest more excellent performance in the 1 M KOH electrolyte with \(0.8\,\mathrm{mM}\) CLs than Ru NPs, and meanwhile possess smaller electrochemical impedance, sufficient confirming the role of carboxylate ligand on enhancing HER kinetics.
Long-term durability is another important parameter for the practical application of catalyst. As shown in Figure S15, the LSV curve of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) after 5000 cycles almost remains coincident with the initial curve with a very small potential increase of only about \(1.0\,\mathrm{mV}\) at \(50\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\). Furthermore, a long-term electrocatalytic HER process on \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) was performed at a constant current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and the chronopotentiometric curve was recorded for \(80\,\mathrm{h}\) as
shown in Figure S16, which shows the attenuation of overpotential is only \(\sim8\,\mathrm{mV}\) after \(80\,\mathrm{h}\) test, suggesting much high stability. After HER durability test of \(80\,\mathrm{h}\), SEM, TEM and XPS characterizations of \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) all proved that the surface morphology and elemental compositions showed almost no variation as shown in Figure S17-S19, suggesting high structure stability of the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) during HER process.
