## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we prepared the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) catalyst, which is used as a platform to reveal the potential synergistical mechanism of hydrogen evolution. Experimental results combining with DFT calculations disclosed that the carboxylic group in \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) could be utilized as the proton transfer mediator to promote the adsorption/activation/dissociation of water and accordingly to reduce reaction energy barrier and optimize binding energy of hydrogen intermediates. Owing to the synergistic effects between Ru NPs and Ni-MOFs, the \(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni-MOFs/NF}\) catalyst exhibits the excellent catalytic performance with a very low overpotential of \(25\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), and it can even deliver an ultrahigh current density of \(500\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\) at the overpotential of \(160\;\mathrm{mV}\). This catalyst also exhibits excellent long-term durability for at least \(80\;\mathrm{h}\) with only \(\sim8\;\mathrm{mV}\) loss in the overpotential. This study not only provides a novel alkaline HER electrocatalyst with remarkable catalytic performance but also will open a new avenue for the rational design of other catalysts.
